.TH PADNUM 1 "2026" "padnum @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
padnum \- zero\-pad the numeric prefix of filenames, with undo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B padnum
[\fB\-p\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-sep\fR \fISTRING\fR] [\fB\-\-ext\fR \fIEXT\fR] [\fB\-n\fR]
.br
.B padnum
\fB\-\-undo\fR [\fIN\fR]
.br
.B padnum
\fB\-\-history\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B padnum
works on the files in the current directory. For every filename that starts
with digits it zero\-pads that number to a common width and leaves the rest of
the name exactly as it was \(em Arabic text, spaces, underscores and dots all
survive untouched.
.PP
So \fB1 intro.mp4\fR, \fB2 setup.mp4\fR ... \fB10 wrap.mp4\fR become
\fB01 intro.mp4\fR, \fB02 setup.mp4\fR ... \fB10 wrap.mp4\fR, which is what any
sane sort order needs.
.PP
Files that do not start with a digit are left alone, and so are directories.
Leading separators between the number and the rest of the name
(space, underscore, dot, hyphen) are normalised to the separator you choose.
.SS Padding width
Without
.BR \-p ,
the width is detected from the largest number present: 9 files gives width 1,
10 files gives width 2, 100 gives 3.
.SS Undo
Every run records what it renamed in
.B .padnum.undo
in the working directory, keeping up to 10 generations. A dry run records
nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-padding " " \fIN\fR
Pad to a fixed width of \fIN\fR digits instead of detecting it.
.TP
.B \-\-sep " " \fISTRING\fR
Separator between the number and the rest of the name. Default is a single
space.
.TP
.B \-\-ext " " \fIEXT\fR
Only touch files with this extension, matched case\-insensitively.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be renamed and change nothing, including the undo history.
.TP
.B \-\-undo " " [\fIN\fR]
Undo the last operation, or the last \fIN\fR operations. Combine with
.B \-n
to see what an undo would do.
.TP
.B \-\-history
Show the undo history, oldest first, in a pager.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./.padnum.undo
Undo history for the directory it sits in. Delete it to forget the history.
.SH EXAMPLES
Pad using the detected width:
.PP
.RS
.EX
padnum
.EE
.RE
.PP
Force three digits, only for mp4 files, and look before you leap:
.PP
.RS
.EX
padnum \-n \-p 3 \-\-ext mp4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a hyphen instead of a space, then change your mind:
.PP
.RS
.EX
padnum \-\-sep "\-"
padnum \-\-undo
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No undo history, or no more undo steps available.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mv (1),
.BR rename (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by @MAINTAINER@.
